          {
            "x": 2.23463687150838,
            "y": 13.245033112582782,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>The Discovery of Precession</strong><br>\nOne of Hipparchus's most notable discoveries was the precession of the equinoxes. He noticed that the positions of the stars had shifted over time, which was not consistent with the prevailing belief that stars were fixed in their positions. This discovery changed the way people understood the dynamics of the cosmos.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Star Catalog</strong><br>\nIn order to track the movement of the stars, Hipparchus created one of the first star catalogs in the Western world. He classified stars by their brightness and noted their positions, providing a resource for astronomers for years to come. This was a monumental task that required extraordinary precision.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Trigonometry and Hipparchus</strong><br>\nHipparchus is often called the father of trigonometry. He developed a method to solve problems involving triangles that was revolutionary for the time. His work with trigonometry was essential for making more accurate astronomical predictions and maps.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "3b13867b-feab-4e2e-bfa2-fcc82e4dbff1"
            },
            "alwaysDisplayComments": false,
            "backgroundOpacity": 0,
            "displayAsButton": false,
            "buttonSize": "big",
