          {
            "x": 2.178649237472767,
            "y": 12.910611231371064,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>The Lunar Theory</strong><br>\nHipparchus's lunar theory explained the moon's apparent motion in the sky. He suggested that the path of the moon is elliptical, not circular, accounting for the speed variations in its orbit. This theory was a significant advancement over previous models.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Geography and Mapmaking</strong><br>\nAside from his astronomical pursuits, Hipparchus was also interested in geography. He applied his knowledge of astronomy to improve the accuracy of maps. By using the positions of the sun and stars, he could calculate latitude and longitude, helping to determine the geographic location of different places.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Legacy of Hipparchus</strong><br>\nHipparchus's work had a lasting impact on both astronomy and mathematics. His methods laid the groundwork for future scientists like Ptolemy, and his discoveries continued to influence astronomical research for centuries. Even today, his work is recognized for its extraordinary precision and depth.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "3d655444-fd55-42f7-aba2-16a16db7dc58"
